---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "@/components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Data table"
  description="A docs-only data table recipe built from table, checkbox, badge and button."
>
  <div class="docs-content">
    <p class="docs-eyebrow">Recipes</p>
    <h1>Data table</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      A data table is a native table with selection, status and row actions. Sorting,
      filtering, pagination and bulk actions stay in consumer JavaScript.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout" role="note">
      <strong>Recipe, not a registry item.</strong> Install the composition pieces with
      <code>loseta add table checkbox badge button</code>. There is no data-table class
      to install.
    </div>

    <h2>Invoices</h2>
    <ComponentPreview
      id="recipe-data-table"
      title="Default"
      html={`<div class="table-container" style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); overflow: hidden">
  <table class="table">
    <caption class="sr-only">Recent invoices</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col"><input class="checkbox" type="checkbox" aria-label="Select all invoices"></th>
        <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending">Invoice</th>
        <th scope="col">Status</th>
        <th scope="col">Method</th>
        <th scope="col">Amount</th>
        <th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Actions</span></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><input class="checkbox" type="checkbox" aria-label="Select INV-001"></td>
        <td>INV-001</td>
        <td><span class="badge" data-variant="outline">Paid</span></td>
        <td>Credit card</td>
        <td>$250.00</td>
        <td><button type="button" class="btn" data-variant="ghost" data-size="icon-sm" aria-label="Open actions for INV-001"><span aria-hidden="true">···</span></button></td>
      </tr>
      <tr aria-selected="true">
        <td><input class="checkbox" type="checkbox" aria-label="Select INV-002" checked></td>
        <td>INV-002</td>
        <td><span class="badge" data-variant="secondary">Pending</span></td>
        <td>PayPal</td>
        <td>$150.00</td>
        <td><button type="button" class="btn" data-variant="ghost" data-size="icon-sm" aria-label="Open actions for INV-002"><span aria-hidden="true">···</span></button></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><input class="checkbox" type="checkbox" aria-label="Select INV-003"></td>
        <td>INV-003</td>
        <td><span class="badge" data-variant="destructive">Unpaid</span></td>
        <td>Bank transfer</td>
        <td>$350.00</td>
        <td><button type="button" class="btn" data-variant="ghost" data-size="icon-sm" aria-label="Open actions for INV-003"><span aria-hidden="true">···</span></button></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>`}
    />

    <h2>Selection</h2>
    <p>
      Use native checkboxes with an accessible name per row. Mark a selected row with
      <code>aria-selected="true"</code>; the table styles the row without requiring a
      custom class. A header checkbox can select the visible page, but the select-all
      state is consumer behavior.
    </p>

    <h2>Sorting</h2>
    <p>
      Put <code>aria-sort="ascending"</code>, <code>"descending"</code> or
      <code>"none"</code> on the active column header and update it when the sort changes.
      The CSS does not draw an arrow, so include a visible indicator or an
      <code>aria-label</code> that explains the current order.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="html"
      code={`<th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending">
  Invoice
  <span class="sr-only">sorted ascending</span>
</th>`}
    />

    <h2>Row actions</h2>
    <p>
      Compose the actions trigger with <code>dropdown-menu</code>. The trigger needs an
      accessible name that identifies the row; the menu carries the open state and
      keyboard behavior in consumer JavaScript.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="html"
      code={`<button class="btn" data-variant="ghost" data-size="icon-sm"
  aria-label="Open actions for INV-002" aria-expanded="false">
  <span aria-hidden="true">···</span>
</button>`}
    />

    <h2>Composition notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Put the table in <code>.table-container</code> so narrow screens can scroll it.</li>
      <li>Use <code>&lt;th scope="col"&gt;</code> for headers and a <code>&lt;caption&gt;</code> for the table name.</li>
      <li>Do not hide the table header to save space; keep the column meaning available.</li>
      <li>Pagination is a separate composition with <code>pagination</code>; it is not built into the table.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
